<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Some of this criticism kind of rubs me the wrong way. I think this is what's bothering me:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I think the concept works, but the actions of D-Class blamed on this thing are really clashing with how I imagine the Foundation operates.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you come up with a theory about what's going on, and then discover that the article clashes with the theory, that doesn't necessarily mean the article is contradictory. It could just mean that your theory isn't supported by the text, and that something else is going on. Several of your bullet points are explained by this. It's not a matter of the article being consistent, but there being evidence against your interpretation within the text. Perhaps a different theory aligns more closely to what is presented; that kind of experience is what mysteries like this are supposed to provoke.</p>
<p>Other assorted points:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also, should this thing not be Keter? They seem to lack any real system of containment.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As the article mentions, all hostile activity has ceased. As far as they can tell, it isn't doing anything anymore and neutralization is imminent.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the universe of me as the reader of this article, you establish me as the Administrator, or someone with the same level of clearance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This whole point is explained pretty simply: logging in as Administrator comes <em>after</em> the display of the article, and in what is indicated to be an entirely separate console. As such, it's a weird point to be such of an issue.</p>
</div>
