<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>But as the author, you need to ensure you're getting your ideas across in a succinct and understandable way. I was confused as to what was really going on for a lot of this.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See, this is fair criticism, but it's entirely separate from your original approach, which criticized aspects of the article that contradicted your theory. The former is helpful, the latter less so.</p>
<p>Often part of the fun of articles like this is community engagement. If you're having trouble figuring things out, I'd suggest looking at some of the other comments, which may offer some insight.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Classification is not dependent on how dangerous or hostile something is. It is about how difficult it is to contain. If, until it is neutralized, they have no way to truly contain it, it's Keter.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's not how classification usually works. It's not solely one thing or solely another, it's a mixture of ease of containment and threat to human life. As an anomaly that is not <em>physically</em> contained but poses no apparent threat of affecting normalcy or harming people (i.e. actual containment is easy), it falls firmly into the euclid category. Maybe this is your headcanon, but that's not how it's usually written.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And you'd be logged in with the same credentials the whole time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Clearly the way the article is written shows that this is not the case. Is it really so beyond belief that the in-fiction system might work this way that it creates a massive problem for your immersion? Obviously if that <em>is</em> how you approach articles I can't really change it, but it sounds like a poor barrier to enjoyment.</p>
</div>
